From: Project Arctic
Subject: winebus: an interface with a vendor-defined collection beside a mouse's or keyboard's comes from hidraw.

Wine takes mice and keyboards from evdev and drops their hidraw nodes,
judging by the first top-level collection only. A G102's second interface
has a keyboard collection first and Logitech's HID++ channel (usage page
0xFF00) after it; through evdev that channel was gone, and G HUB showed no
device ("Connect Your Logitech Gear"). Windows shows every collection. An
interface with a vendor-defined collection is now taken from hidraw; plain
mice and keyboards stay on evdev. Verified: a HID++ 2.0 ping on the
collection is answered (protocol 4.2), G HUB shows the G102 and its DPI.
---
diff --git a/dlls/winebus.sys/main.c b/dlls/winebus.sys/main.c
index 5f46932..0de9f1e 100644
--- a/dlls/winebus.sys/main.c
+++ b/dlls/winebus.sys/main.c
@@ -931,6 +931,19 @@ static USAGE_AND_PAGE get_device_usages(UINT64 unix_device, UINT *buttons)
         usages.UsagePage = device_desc.CollectionDesc[0].UsagePage;
         usages.Usage = device_desc.CollectionDesc[0].Usage;
 
+        /* Arctic: an interface whose first collection is a mouse's or a
+         * keyboard's can have a vendor-defined one beside it: the channel
+         * a mouse's software talks to it through (Logitech's HID++ on a G102's
+         * second interface). Windows shows each collection; through evdev
+         * that one is lost, so such an interface is taken from hidraw. */
+        for (i = 1; i < device_desc.CollectionDescLength; i++)
+        {
+            if (device_desc.CollectionDesc[i].UsagePage < 0xff00) continue;
+            usages.UsagePage = device_desc.CollectionDesc[i].UsagePage;
+            usages.Usage = device_desc.CollectionDesc[i].Usage;
+            break;
+        }
+
         if ((status = HidP_GetCaps(preparsed, &caps)) == HIDP_STATUS_SUCCESS &&
             (button_caps = malloc(sizeof(*button_caps) * caps.NumberInputButtonCaps)))
         {
